`lib/WWW/PayPal/Role/HTTP.pm` (`request`, the `unless ($res->is_success)` block) croaks `"PayPal API error ($method $path): $msg"` where `$msg` is only `message` / `error_description` / `error` of the body.

A PayPal error body carries `name` (`UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY`), `details[].issue` (`INSTRUMENT_DECLINED`, `ORDER_ALREADY_CAPTURED`, `DUPLICATE_INVOICE_ID`, ...) and `debug_id`. Callers have to branch on `issue` (PayPal's own guidance: on `INSTRUMENT_DECLINED` restart the approval, on `ORDER_ALREADY_CAPTURED` treat the capture as done) and PayPal support asks for `debug_id`. None of that survives; the only way to tell a declined card from a duplicate capture is to regex the generic `message`.

Related in the same block:
- `decode_json($body)` runs before the status check without `eval`, so an error (or a truncated success) whose body starts with `{` but is not valid JSON dies with a JSON::XS message instead of an API error.
- A network failure (LWP's internal 500) is reported as `PayPal API error (...): 500 Can't connect ...`, indistinguishable from a real 500.
- `_fetch_token` likewise `decode_json`s unguarded and, with a missing `expires_in`, sets the expiry to `time - 60`, i.e. fetches a new token on every request.
- There is no retry on a 401 from a token PayPal has invalidated early.

Done when: `request` throws an exception object (stringifying to the current message, so existing `$@ =~` matching keeps working) with `status`, `name`, `issue`/`details`, `debug_id` and the decoded body, network failures are a distinct class, and `t/` has an offline test for a 422 `INSTRUMENT_DECLINED` body.

